tributes to sir david amos have come in  from across the political divide with  many pointing to his commitment to the  local area and his constituents i want  to speak now to two members of  parliament from the essex region the  conservative mp for romford andrew  rossendell who's been friends with sir  david for 40 years and the labour mp for  ilford north west streeting  thank you gentlemen for joining us it's  unusual for us to have two members of  parliament on together especially from  different  parties but i'd like you both to reflect  on your  experience not as partisans but as  constituency mps  who  were close to sir david  and i just would like starting with you  andrew  if you want to say something about your  memories of him  david was a wonderful person he was a  really genuine friend  we've all got friends in parliament he  was special because he really cared  about  the people around him he cared about his  constituency  he was a passionate person and he had  strong beliefs he he was uncompromising  what he stood for  but the things he stood for  were wonderful causes for instance  animal welfare he was  he was a huge animal welfare supporter  and  the other thing i'd say about him is  that  he would  always have time for everybody he met  you know he didn't just brush you off he  he would spend time talking to all all  the people he met and nothing would be  too much he never looked down on anyone  he was he he was an equal to everyone  else that's the way he he behaved and  we're going to miss him he was a  fantastic mp and a great friend and  for him to lose him in this such tragic  circumstances um  it's very difficult for us to accept and  understand it really is it's a very sad  moment for all of us  where's streeting  yeah i think we were just discussing  before we came on um david was one of  those great characters in parliament  everyone knew him because you could not  know him um he was  renowned for as andrew said the cause he  took up  a terrific sense of humor and also just  the most outstanding champion for his  constituency and i've not checked but i  would put money on the fact that no one  has ever mentioned south end in  parliament more than sir david did and  i'm really glad actually that the  campaign for city status for south end  is um you know really up at the top of  the agenda because i could think of no  more fitting tribute to him and he was  loved right across the political  spectrum and you've heard that in recent  days from some of my labor colleagues  who absolutely love david loved working  for him and i don't think that  there's any real comfort that we could  offer his family today but i hope they  draw some small comfort from just how  much  um you know a husband a father was just  loved right across the political  spectrum in the house of commons and  evidently loved by his constituents as  we've seen in the outpouring of grief  and love for david in the last day or so  the conversation this morning has  centered very much on the security  of members of parliament and uh i would  guess also local councillors who have to  meet people they don't know face to face  and there's some suggestion that  actually over recent times things have  become  more dangerous  uh  certainly rougher  has either of you  have you had to take  extra precautions over your period uh as  members of parliament andrew rossendale  first  um yes i'm afraid  i've been mp 20 years and during that  period  uh i see  a lot of more nasty people around than  ever before and they're willing to say  and do things  in a way that i would never thought  possible in this country and we all have  to be really aware and keep our wits  about us the abuse we get on social  media by emails i mean we get it all the  time but we brush it off because we've  got a job to do and  we tell the police the police have got  other things to do it's not always  followed up  and i think probably this is all going  to change we've got to take this a bit  more seriously in future i've had my  office was arsoned my car was smashed up  someone tried to get into my house and  injure me managed to stop them all these  things have happened over the years but  you take it in your stride because  our priority is to to get on with our  job and we we we do tend to not think  much about our own security i'm afraid  but i'm thinking now maybe we should do  a bit more um  pretty patel's reviewing  how we're going to be how this is going  to be dealt with in future certainly our  constituency surgeries there's going to  be have to be some new arrangements for  those but we can't let evil destroy  democracy and this was pure evil what  happened here we cannot allow that to  undermine the democratic traditions of  this country so whatever happens  where's myself and all members of  parliament we're going to be out there  serving our constituents we're not going  to let this defeat us we'll still be  there and doing the job we're elected to  do  we're streeting andrew rosendale says we  have to do a bit more  what in your view would a bit more in  practical terms constitute  i think it's a good question trevor and  i don't know what the answer is to be  honest um  i think the thing about the  accessibility of politicians in britain  and it's when i've met politicians from  other countries they they remark upon it  it's not just that we do it because we  feel obliged to and we think it's part  of the job we do it because we love  doing it we love spending time in our  communities we love spending time with  our constituents um we you know we're  people people we like spending time with  people that's why we're in politics to  make a difference and to serve people so  so i think  none of us really want to change that we  don't want to take measures that that  undermine that but we're having  difficult conversations with our own  families this is not just about us it's  about the safety of our staff the people  who work for us it's about you know how  our families feel when we go off to work  and the conversation that particularly  you know our colleagues who are parents  have been having with their children  in the last day or so about why our jobs  important and why it's worth the risk  but you know this is the second  second  incident like this in five years and you  know it's we've been stricken with grief  for david grief for joe  and you know and also grief for the job  that we do in a sense because  you know  we love it we love what we do  david was the epitome of loving public  service  um so we know that something's got to  change but it you know i think  it's so raw and we don't want to kind of  rush to kind of  snap judgments but we know something's  got to change  there's a lot of talk about reviews and  consideration and so on but  are we going to be here again in  three years four years five years  uh as frankly we were on to  the joe cox murder where everybody said  we have things have to change  but actually  it's hard to  identify a practical measure that has  been taken  wes  i i really hope not  you know the truth is that we  we are exposed every day to risk but  i want to take it serious but i also  think that there are lots of people who  expose to risk every day who serve the  public  we're not unique in that sense and we're  not a special case  um  and i don't want people watching your  program this morning who would be  outstanding public servants whether as  counsellors or members of parliament or  working in other public-facing roles to  think this is no longer for me i don't  want to do that i i can't tell you  trevor how much i love doing this job um  and i know i speak for andrew on this  because you know  lived in your constituency at one point  parliamentary neighbours even though  andrew drives around my constituency  every election with his loud hailer  trying to get me out you know i can sit  around you a cross-party friend um we  love doing this job absolutely love it  and i wouldn't want anyone to be put off  but part of that has got to be i'm sure  making sure we're safe  andrew rosendale  um  many people would say that  part of what we are seeing now  is an atmosphere that has generated by  a heightened rhetoric  and the way that people talk about talk  about and to each other in politics at  not least members of parliament  themselves to what extent  do you think that this changed  atmosphere actually  uh is the responsibility of  parliamentarians themselves call you  know people who call others enemies of  the people people who call other people  scum for example  so  we're all in politics and we accept that  we are going to be criticized for our  views not everyone agrees with our  opinions and i don't mind a political  debate i don't mind people attacking me  because they disagree with my views on  whatever subject it may be however there  is a level to which that's it is  acceptable  to have that kind of debate if you use  nasty hateful language if you if you use  language which is going to  place you in a position whereby you look  like you  you hate that person because their views  that's not acceptable and there are  some politicians who have said things  they shouldn't have said  um i don't think that's been the cause  of this though it may be part of the  background to i don't know we'll find  out in due course but we we all have a  responsibility to show respect for each  other to show kindness um despite our  political differences and where's the  probably example of that you know we're  neighboring seats we've never had a bad  word to say about each other that quite  the opposite  but we have different views on issues um  we always will because we're different  parties but it doesn't stop us being  friends and colleagues it doesn't stop  us working together on issues and we  agree on far more than the public think  actually uh it's amazing how many mps on  other side of the house that i agree  with on issues and it's not all it's not  all um  bad feeling between us and sometimes the  media can project it like we're all at  each other but we're not  there's a lot of things that we agree on  work together on  but we've got them be careful of our  language and we've all got to make sure  that when we speak in public and when we  tweet things out and when we issue  statements that that we show respect for  other people even if they're  diametrically opposed to our particular  point of view  we're streeting um  i  remember  after  joe cox died  much of the language that we're hearing  this weekend of kindness and  more in common than we that separates us  and so on being used and people  promising  that they would be more respectful and  so on um  i think it took roughly six weeks for  members of parliament to be yelling at  each other across the  aisle again  now  you know you two  are not known particularly for that kind  of thing  what can we actually do  to make sure that all of the good  intentions that are going to be  expressed today and tomorrow and the day  after  last into next year  well it takes it takes work and it takes  um some effort and um you know i think  you're a bit too generous there i can i  think if you ask the speaker or  successive speakers i've got quite a  loud voice in the commons chamber and  the irony is as much as people say they  don't like the the the jeering and the  yelling at prime minister's questions  it's the most watched part of the week  um  i i i think that  the the the the rough and humble of  politics the you know the the argue  bhaji of debate that's part of it i  think there are times when you know  intemperate language can go too far and  i wish that it wouldn't take awful  tragedies like this for us to  remind ourselves and particularly to  remind the public that we do actually  quite like each other actually across  the political spectrum there are lots of  cross-party friendships and as i say  whenever i do a school visit in my  constituency when i look across the  commons chamber to conservative mps i  don't see a bunch of people who are evil  i see people who like me are motivated  by a commitment to public service and  wanting to make our country a better  place we just differ on how to do that  and that's what politics is all about  and and i think we can make that point  uh more often without losing robust  debate i think robust debate is a  healthy part of democracy um but i think  i think perhaps we might impugn each  other's motives a little less um  and also i mean i think we've also got  to  it's always a time to talk about  civility and politics i think we've also  got to reflect on the specific  circumstances of as to how two members  of parliament um have lost their lives  in this way and i think that's about  more than civility it's about extremism  we've got to tackle that too  right we're streeting andrew rosendale  thank you very much indeed for joining  us

